nce nets out to a narrow Republican edge with wide uncertainty, keeping this in tossup territory.", "watch": "Whether independent, non-partisan polling emerges in the final weeks, and how the Bevin-era attacks on Alvarado and the affordability message land with Lexington-area swing voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Andy Barr (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kentucky's 6th District is an open seat after Andy Barr's retirement to run for Senate, and both parties see a real opportunity. The district leans Republican by about seven points, but Democrats are competing hard: Governor Beshear has made flipping the seat a priority, and Democratic-sponsored polls through September show Zach Dembo roughly even with or slightly ahead of Ralph Alvarado. Republicans point to the district's historical patterns and Alvarado's healthcare background.\n\nThe race has turned negative, with fact-checks examining attacks on Alvarado's record under the Bevin administration and on Dembo's immigration positions. Outside spending is flowing to both candidates, though neither holds a clear financial edge. Forecasters mostly rate the seat Lean Republican, with some calling it Likely R, while prediction markets give Alvarado a clear but not commanding advantage.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2515, "margin": -3.92}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.296, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2877, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2982, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.223, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2389, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2735, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2956, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2849, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.3514, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.3215, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.3486, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-1